---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Nav from '../../components/Nav.astro';
import Footer from '../../components/Footer.astro';
import Sitrep from '../../components/Sitrep.astro';

const pageSchema = [
  {
    '@type': 'WebPage',
    '@id': 'https://sitrep.md/vs/nimbalyst/#page',
    url: 'https://sitrep.md/vs/nimbalyst/',
    name: 'sitrep vs Nimbalyst - workspace or docs reader',
    description:
      'A visual workspace you actively run AI coding sessions inside, versus an AI agent docs reader for the markdown those sessions leave behind.',
    isPartOf: { '@id': 'https://sitrep.md/#website' },
  },
];

const rows = [
  {
    dim: 'What it is',
    sitrep: 'AI agent docs reader: the docs your agents wrote, from every repo, in one list, with status, open decisions and staleness parsed out',
    other: 'A visual workspace and session manager for AI coding agents (successor to Crystal)',
  },
  {
    dim: 'How you use it',
    sitrep: 'Read in it: the docs your sessions left behind, whatever tool ran them',
    other: 'Work inside it - run sessions, review diffs, edit documents in its own editors',
  },
  {
    dim: 'Parses organic agent markdown',
    sitrep: 'Yes - status, phases, decisions and due dates from files nobody structured for a tool',
    other: 'No - it tracks its own sessions and tasks, with WYSIWYG markdown and diagram editors',
  },
  {
    dim: 'Scope',
    sitrep: 'Cross-project: one list across every repo and workspace on disk',
    other: 'The workspaces and sessions you run inside it',
  },
  {
    dim: 'Runs agents',
    sitrep: 'Never - orchestration is on the anti-roadmap',
    other: 'Yes - parallel sessions with diff review are the core feature',
  },
  {
    dim: 'Editing',
    sitrep: 'A reader, not an editor: three guarded state operations are the only writes',
    other: 'Visual editors are part of the product',
  },
  {
    dim: 'Mobile',
    sitrep: 'Mobile sync, a closed preview not open to users yet: a read-only, end-to-end-encrypted mirror on your phone',
    other: 'iOS companion app',
  },
  {
    dim: 'License',
    sitrep: 'Commercial Mac app: €49 / $49 one-time, a year of updates included, keep it forever',
    other: 'Open source (MIT), free for individuals, with a team tier',
  },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="sitrep vs Nimbalyst - workspace or docs reader"
  description="Nimbalyst is a visual workspace you run AI coding sessions inside; sitrep is an AI agent docs reader for the markdown those sessions leave behind. When to choose each, including when Nimbalyst is the better pick."
  schema={pageSchema}
>
  <Nav />

  <main class="mx-auto w-full max-w-5xl px-6">
    <header class="flex flex-col gap-4 pt-16 pb-12 sm:pt-20">
      <p class="text-xs font-medium tracking-widest text-accent uppercase">Comparison</p>
      <h1 class="max-w-3xl text-3xl font-semibold tracking-tight sm:text-4xl">
        <Sitrep /> vs Nimbalyst
      </h1>
      <p class="max-w-prose text-lg text-ink-muted">
        Nimbalyst is a serious, well-built visual workspace for running
        Claude Code and Codex sessions - open source, actively developed,
        with an iOS companion. The job here is different, and the fastest
        way to pick is to notice which direction you face: Nimbalyst is
        where you work; <Sitrep /> is where you read what the work left
        behind.
      </p>
      <p class="max-w-prose rounded-md border border-line bg-surface-raised px-4 py-3 text-sm text-ink-muted">
        Disclosure: this page is written by <a href="/about/" class="text-accent underline underline-offset-4 focus-visible:outline-2 focus-visible:outline-offset-2 focus-visible:outline-accent">the maker of sitrep</a>. Read the
        framing with that in mind - and note that where the honest answer is
        "use Nimbalyst", this page says so.
      </p>
    </header>

    <section class="border-t border-line py-12 sm:py-16" aria-labelledby="difference">
      <h2 id="difference" class="text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight">The one-sentence difference</h2>
      <div class="mt-5 flex max-w-prose flex-col gap-4 text-ink-muted">
        <p>
          Nimbalyst is a workspace you operate inside: it runs parallel agent
          sessions, shows you their diffs, and gives you visual editors for
          the documents around them. <Sitrep /> is an AI agent docs reader
          for the markdown your agents already wrote. It runs nothing, edits
          nothing, and works the same whichever tool produced the files.
        </p>
        <p>
          The two answer different moments. Nimbalyst is for the hours you
          are actively driving agents. <Sitrep /> is for reading what they
          wrote: the plan before you approve it, the handoff after the
          session, and, after days away, what moved, what is stalled and what
          is waiting on you. That covers every project, not just the ones in
          a workspace.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="border-t border-line py-12 sm:py-16" aria-labelledby="table">
      <h2 id="table" class="text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight">Side by side</h2>
      <div class="mt-8 overflow-x-auto">
        <table class="w-full min-w-[40rem] border-collapse text-sm">
          <thead>
            <tr class="border-b border-line text-left">
              <th scope="col" class="py-3 pr-4 font-medium"></th>
              <th scope="col" class="py-3 pr-4 font-medium"><Sitrep /></th>
              <th scope="col" class="py-3 font-medium">Nimbalyst</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {
              rows.map(({ dim, sitrep, other }) => (
                <tr class="border-b border-line align-top">
                  <th scope="row" class="py-3 pr-4 text-left font-medium">{dim}</th>
                  <td class="py-3 pr-4 text-ink-muted">{sitrep}</td>
                  <td class="py-3 text-ink-muted">{other}</td>
                </tr>
              ))
            }
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="border-t border-line py-12 sm:py-16" aria-labelledby="choose-nimbalyst">
      <h2 id="choose-nimbalyst" class="text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight">When to choose Nimbalyst</h2>
      <ul class="mt-6 grid max-w-2xl gap-3 text-ink-muted">
        <li class="flex gap-3 text-sm">
          <span aria-hidden="true" class="text-accent select-none">&rarr;</span>
          You want to run several agent sessions in parallel and review
          their diffs in one place - active orchestration is the job.
        </li>
        <li class="flex gap-3 text-sm">
          <span aria-hidden="true" class="text-accent select-none">&rarr;</span>
          You like working in a visual environment: WYSIWYG markdown,
          diagrams, a session board.
        </li>
        <li class="flex gap-3 text-sm">
          <span aria-hidden="true" class="text-accent select-none">&rarr;</span>
          You want open source you can read and extend, free for individual
          use, from an active team.
        </li>
        <li class="flex gap-3 text-sm">
          <span aria-hidden="true" class="text-accent select-none">&rarr;</span>
          You want a companion app for keeping an eye on live sessions from
          an iPhone.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p class="mt-6 max-w-prose text-sm text-ink-muted">
        <Sitrep /> will never compete on any of that: running agents is on its
        anti-roadmap permanently. If orchestration is what you need,
        Nimbalyst is a good place to get it.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="border-t border-line py-12 sm:py-16" aria-labelledby="choose-sitrep">
      <h2 id="choose-sitrep" class="text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight">When to choose <Sitrep /></h2>
      <ul class="mt-6 grid max-w-2xl gap-3 text-ink-muted">
        <li class="flex gap-3 text-sm">
          <span aria-hidden="true" class="text-accent select-none">&rarr;</span>
          Your question is not "how do I run agents" but "what did they
          write, and where do my projects stand", especially after time
          away.
        </li>
        <li class="flex gap-3 text-sm">
          <span aria-hidden="true" class="text-accent select-none">&rarr;</span>
          Your state lives in the documents agents leave behind - plans,
          decision logs, analyses - not in session history, and you want it
          parsed rather than re-read.
        </li>
        <li class="flex gap-3 text-sm">
          <span aria-hidden="true" class="text-accent select-none">&rarr;</span>
          You work across many repos and tools - some sessions in a
          terminal, some in an IDE, some on another machine - and want one
          overview that does not care where the work happened.
        </li>
        <li class="flex gap-3 text-sm">
          <span aria-hidden="true" class="text-accent select-none">&rarr;</span>
          You want a tool that never runs, edits or restructures anything: a
          reader, not a second cockpit.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="border-t border-line py-12 sm:py-16" aria-labelledby="both">
      <h2 id="both" class="text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight">Using both</h2>
      <p class="mt-5 max-w-prose text-ink-muted">
        There is no migration between these tools, because <Sitrep /> demands
        nothing: point it at the folders your Nimbalyst workspaces (and
        everything else) write into, and it starts parsing state from the
        markdown that is already there. Drive sessions in Nimbalyst; open
        <Sitrep /> to read what they wrote, or when you come back after days
        away. Neither tool has to know the other
        exists - which is the whole idea behind <a href="/principles/" class="text-accent underline underline-offset-4 focus-visible:outline-2 focus-visible:outline-offset-2 focus-visible:outline-accent">zero adoption cost</a>.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 max-w-prose text-sm text-ink-faint">
        <Sitrep /> is available now on macOS; the 14-day trial is the full
        product. Local-first - your files never leave your machine.
      </p>
    </section>
  </main>

  <Footer />
</BaseLayout>
